Day 232/250: Minimum Time Visiting All Points

Problem Statement #1266

Problem Statement Explanation:

On a 2D plane, there are n points with integer coordinates points[i] = [xi, yi]. 
Your task is to find the minimum time in seconds to visit all points in the order given by points.

Rules for movement:

In one second, you can move:
Vertically: 1 unit
Horizontally: 1 unit
Diagonally: 1 unit (this is the key to minimizing time)
You must visit the points in the specific order they appear in the array.

Solution Apprroach:

The core of this problem lies in understanding that diagonal movement is the most efficient way to travel. 
When moving between two points (x^1,y^1) and (x^2,y^2):

Diagonal Advantage: Moving diagonally covers one unit of both x and y simultaneously in just 1 second.
The Formula: The minimum time to move between two points is determined by the dimension with the largest gap. 
This is known as the Chebyshev Distance.
Calculation: For any two consecutive points, the time taken is:

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public int minTimeToVisitAllPoints(int[][] points) {
        int totalTime = 0;
        
        for (int i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
            int x1 = points[i][0];
            int y1 = points[i][1];
            
            int x2 = points[i + 1][0];
            int y2 = points[i + 1][1];
            
            int diffX = Math.abs(x2 - x1);
            int diffY = Math.abs(y2 - y1);
            
            totalTime += Math.max(diffX, diffY);
        }
        
        return totalTime;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity:	O(N)
Space Complexity: O(1)

